, plus one more where two figures would print alike. V5 then added kernel/numbers.set_places, which places a set at two significant figures of the least figure as rounded, keeps a whole member's decimals, and never caps by the decimals held. numbers.py's SET_FIGURES comment says host pass 6's rule 'already took' O4, but the scorecard was never routed through set_places, and the two rules disagree. A column of whole means 3.0 and 2.0 reads '3' and '2' on the scorecard (decimals held: 0) but '3.0' and '2.0' in Full results (full_results._level_places -> set_places -> 1), which is the very whole-member case the O5 paragraph in numbers.py rules on. A least figure of 0.0996 reads '0.100' on the scorecard (floor(log10) gives 3) but '0.10' in Full results and in a figure key (figure_keys.figure_places -> set_places -> 2; the test pins set_places([0.0996]) == 2). A reader comparing the first screen with Full results sees one value in two forms, which contradicts V5's stated 'one rule in kernel/numbers.py, applied everywhere a figure prints'. Fix by construction: have scorecard._one_precision call set_places, keeping any scorecard-only tie-break as a documented exception; or record the scorecard's divergence as an owner-ruled exception. Then add one test that puts the same measure's figure on the scorecard and in Full results and asserts the same text. Search: any other hand-rolled 'least figure' places (log10/floor on a set) under analysis/report.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/campaign/scorecard.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/kernel/numbers.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/parts/full_results.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/figure_keys.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "set_places has no floor for float noise: a change of ~1e-17 in a measure's set spells every member of the column (and its figure key) to ~18 decimals", "recommendation": "Scope: instance, at the one owner. set_places drops only None, non-finite and exact zeros, and it is uncapped. A contrast's delta is mean_b - mean_a (bundle/comparisons.py), and with float measures it can be pure residue. comparisons.py itself carries an untested reason for 'values differ by less than floating point resolves'. For a set holding 5.5e-17, set_places returns 2-1+17 = 18. full_results._change_places, build._reading_places(changes) and figure_keys.figure_places(change=True) then spell every change of that measure at 18 decimals ('+1.200000000000000000 s'). The tiny member reads '0.00000000000000006' rather than as its bound, because spell_at falls back to the '< 0.000001' bound only when the text rounds to zero. ends_places already guards this exact case ('float noise of 1e-9 never spells the interval to nine places') and caps at _WRITTEN_PLACES; set_places does neither. Fix in set_places: ignore members with abs(value) < WRITTEN_FROM, as ends_places does, and cap the result at _WRITTEN_PLACES. Add a test: set_places([1.2, 5.5e-17]) == 1.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/kernel/numbers.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_presentation_number_forms.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "The recorded test evidence names its one failure by count only, so it cannot be confirmed as the expected minor-bump red", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. prawduct-hook test-status exits 1 with 'stale: 1 test(s) failing in saved evidence'. .test-evidence.json (13:01:34Z, after HEAD's 12:56:47 commit) was written with '--from-counts passed=27237 failed=1 skipped=13' and names no failing test id. The dispatch expects test_api_growth_requires_a_minor_bump to be the one red, and this review does not count it as a defect. The record cannot show that the failure is that test rather than a merge-seam regression. Record the failing node id with the counts when evidence is written from counts, so the one red is checkable. No code change.", "files": [".prawduct/.test-evidence.json"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "kit/API.md contradicts itself and the kit on label and note fallback: the tag row says the tag stands alone and the barsH helper row says long labels are truncated, while the bars kind row, GENERATOR 5.2 and kit.js say a label is never cut or reduced to its tag", "recommendation": "Scope: class. The cause: V4 rewrote the `bars`/`dot_interval` kind rows but left the per-field and per-helper rows that restate the same fallback. Two places in docs/eval-report-kit/kit/API.md still describe the old drawer. (1) The `tag` field row (line 67): \"Where neither `label` nor `short_label` fits, the tag stands alone.\" kit.js `kRowLines`/`kFitLabel` and chart_svg `_row_lines`/`_fit_label` never draw the tag alone. (2) The `barsH(el, w, rows, opt)` helper row (line 117): \"Long labels use `sl` or are truncated\". Nothing truncates now; labels wrap. Its `opt` list also omits `log?`, which barsH reads (kit.js line 255). The `bars` kind row also says \"every note is printed whole\", but barsH and chart_svg `_bars` still replace a note with `opt.fmt(val)` where no side holds it. That value is at the chart's fmt, `+num:0` for points, so a -7.6 pts note can fall back to \"-8\". The `note` field row says this, so the kind row and GENERATOR 5.2 (\"A note is printed whole\") overclaim. A generator author or host reading the field or helper rows expects truncation or a tag-only row, and the kind table says the opposite. The V4 review's \"API.md's stale clause\" fix missed these rows. Remedy: state each fallback once, in the kind row, and make the field and helper rows relational (\"labels as the `bars` kind row says\") instead of restating them. To bound the class, search API.md and GENERATOR.md for 'truncat', 'stands alone', 'printed whole' and 'falls back'.", "files": ["docs/eval-report-kit/kit/API.md", "docs/eval-report-kit/GENERATOR.md", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/kit/kit.js", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/chart_svg.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The set-precision decision (which decimals a measure's figures read at, owner rulings O4/O5) has two owners on the written page: figure_keys.figure_places restates full_results._level_places/_change_places, and figure_keys._measure_means restates parts/charting.held_means", "recommendation": "Scope: class. The cause: V5 made figure keys read 'as the Full results print them' by writing a second copy of the table's rule rather than calling it. The two copies read the same ReportInput. The first copy is parts/full_results.py `_level_places` (set_places over `held_means(...)` times `drawn.factor`) and `_change_places` (set_places over the contrasts matching on `(measure, kind)`, times `drawn.factor`). The second is figure_keys.py `figure_places` (set_places over `_measure_means`, no factor, and changes filtered by `kind != \"judged\"`). Its docstring claims it matches the first. `_measure_means` is a copy of charting `held_means` minus the judged table, and `rubric_score` repeats the same tasks/strata/cells walk a third time. Today the two agree, because every plain unit draws at factor 1.0 (look.quantity) and a measure's contrasts share one kind. Only one Kairo-fixture assertion (test_a_key_reads_a_score_and_a_measure_s_interval_at_the_table_s_precision) holds them together. When a maintainer changes the table's rule (a unit restated at a factor, a new contrast kind, judged means in the set), prose keys silently keep the old precision. That breaks the pass's 'one value, one form' (L7) with no failing test. Remedy: a construction. Keep one function that decides a measure's level places and change places, taking the drawn factor. Have full_results and figure_keys both call it, and have one owner collect a measure's means. Code's own page (build._reading_places, over the bundle) is a separate input and is out of scope for this finding. Search: set_places( call sites under analysis/report.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/figure_keys.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/parts/full_results.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/parts/charting.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "reading-reports.md describes marks and forms the page no longer draws: outcome-bar failure causes as 'solid, then a lighter tone' and lost causes by dashed/dotted edges, a scale line 'Change drawn from -7 to 36.7 pts', and a scorecard zero change as '0 pts'", "recommendation": "Scope: class. The cause: the visual pass edited these paragraphs of packages/evals/docs/reading-reports.md (the scorecard section and 'The marks') but kept their examples, and nothing pins them to a render. (1) The outcome bar: blocks._OUTCOME_STYLES draws each failure cause in --bad under its own texture (hatch, cross, dots, rows, cols) and loss causes as --base/--muted, solid and then textured. It has no 'lighter tone' and no dashed or dotted edges. API.md's Outcomes paragraph states the code correctly. (2) The scale line: a points scale now reads its ends at one decimal (html_parts._Scale.span: '-7.0 to 36.7 pts'). A change matrix's line opens with its glyph_key sentence, then 'Drawn from \u2026'. (3) The scorecard example's '50.0% \u00b7 0 pts (unclear)' and '0% \u00b7 0 pts': blocks.TableCell.amount prints an exact zero change as '0.0 pts' beside '-5.0 pts' (V2 O-10, V5). A reader who checks a page against this guide finds marks it says are not there. Remedy: correct the three descriptions. Pin the scorecard markdown example with a test in test_doc_blocks.py, as 'An aspect's first screen' already is, so the class closes by construction. Search reading-reports.md for other rendered examples.", "files": ["packages/evals/docs/reading-reports.md", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/blocks.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/html_parts.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "A family-corrected interval level travels as one string that three parsers split again: interval_level writes '99.7% (95% across 16 comparisons)', and charts._GLOSSED_LEVEL, chart_svg._GLOSSED_LEVEL and kit.js kLevelHead each regex it apart to place 'interval(s)'", "recommendation": "Scope: class. The cause: the chart data's `level` field holds two values, the level and the family gloss. FAMILY_LEVEL_TEMPLATE (parts/words.py) produces the string and three copies of the same regex (`^(\\S+) (\\(.+\\))$`) take it apart. A change to the template's shape, such as #1127's prose form, makes every chart head read '\u2026 (95% across 16 comparisons) intervals' unless all three move together. test_a_family_corrected_level_reads_plainly_in_the_chart_head holds only the current form. Remedy, if #1127 touches the form: carry the gloss as its own chart field (e.g. `level_gloss`) that the kit and its twin print after 'intervals'. At least, give the regex one public owner in charts.py that chart_svg imports.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/charts.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/chart_svg.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/kit/kit.js", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/parts/charting.py"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "look.figure_text's comment says the keys citing a change in points read it as '-3.0 pts', but figure_keys keeps the lone-figure rule for a points key ('-15'), as a test pins", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The cause: the V5 comment generalised the table's form to the keys. figure_keys._spell's pts path uses number_in, which writes a whole change whole. test_an_interval_key_reads_both_ends_at_one_precision_and_a_lone_change_keeps_the_number_rule asserts '-15', and the owner's O5 ruling keeps lone figures on the number rule. Nothing reads the comment. Remedy: delete 'and the keys citing it' from the comment in parts/look.py figure_text.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/parts/look.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "A choice page's pairs table prints a positive change unsigned ('5.0' under 'Change (pts)') while a negative carries '-' and every other change on the page is signed", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The cause: campaign/scorecard.py's pairs table (~line 1290) passes `round(pair.change, 1)` as a bare float. blocks' column-precision pass then gives it a point's decimal but no sign (TableCell.signed defaults False). _change_rows builds the same kind of cell with `signed=True`. A pair is direction-free (first less second), so a reader cannot tell '5.0' from a magnitude except through the Ahead column. That falls short of the pass's 'every change carries its sign' (L2). Remedy: build the cell as _change_rows does: TableCell(value=change, decimals=1, signed=True).", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/campaign/scorecard.py"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "Host-visible changes from V3, V4, V5 and V6 have no CHANGELOG entry and no MIGRATIONS.toml note: only host pass 6, V1 and V2 are recorded for hosts", "recommendation": "Scope: class. The cause is that each chunk landed as its own pushed head, and only the first two visual chunks wrote their host note. In 278bb302a..HEAD, CHANGELOG.md gains two sections: host pass 6 (scales, links, dashes) and V1 (quiet look). MIGRATIONS.toml gains one note, which covers V1 (emphasis, palette) and V2 (scorecard heads, TableCell.level/signed/figure, TableBlock.glyph_key). Neither file records the following, all of which change what a host reads or pins. (a) V3 a10535fc3: change charts gain the public fields `better`, `dots`, `note` and `zero_label` on the dot-interval chart, plus `short_label`. Zero labels now read 'no change vs P \u00b7 September', with a '\u2190 worse \u00b7 better \u2192' line, and strata/task/lever/config changes draw as dots instead of bars. (b) V4 8f76e09e2: an outcome bar's segments are now shares 0-100 (`fmt` 'pct', domain (0, 100), note '20% passed \u00b7 167 of 836') where they were trial counts. Its title reads 'Outcomes, share of each arm's trials', which test_report_golden_g2 pins. A log axis draws dots, scatter points gain `tag`, and code-page chart titles change. (c) V5 dd048874b and 1459b84e4: every page's number forms move: judged scores at two decimals with '(1\u20135)', '0.0 pts', interval ends at one precision ('[-27.9, 100.0] pts', where it read '100'), '< 0.000001', and figure keys at their table's precision. The quick gate's exit line moves with them (8ec54ba9a). (d) V6 b0b0ab4e3: the writer-eval Scorecard drops its Difference column for two share columns and opens on a share figure. The consequence: MIGRATIONS.toml's own header says a porter reads release notes printed from it, and its notes MUST carry breaking changes that are not a public name. A host such as Discodon, which pins rendered pages and reads served chart JSON, would learn about the palette and the scorecard but not that outcome-bar values changed from counts to percent or that every interval end reads at a new precision. Fix: add one CHANGELOG section with a Hosts line, and one unreleased note in MIGRATIONS.toml, covering (a)-(d). To close the class rather than this instance, a check derived from the goldens would do it: a move in packages/evals/tests/fixtures/hot_path_oracle since the section's `since` tag, with no change to the unreleased `notes`, fails. Every chunk here moved those goldens, so that check would have caught each one.", "files": ["CHANGELOG.md", "packages/evals/MIGRATIONS.toml", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/charts.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/blocks.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/kernel/numbers.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/harness/writer_eval.py"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The span's decisions live only in two gitignored artifacts in another worktree, and shipped code cites 'owner ruling O1/O4/O5' by labels that resolve nowhere in the repo", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Search `ruling O[0-9]` under packages/evals/src and packages/evals/tests: 15 files in this diff, among them kernel/numbers.py, charts.py, blocks.py, scorecard.py, figure_keys.py, parts/look.py and writer_eval.py. The cause: the visual pass and host passes 5-6 were planned and ruled in /opt/3tears/.claude/wt/report-kit/.prawduct/artifacts/reset-visual-pass-plan.md and reset-presentation-build.md. Both files are ignored by .gitignore (`.prawduct/`) and sit in a different worktree (branch report-kit/p0-bundle). The manifest confirms the gap: 'no live plan claims evals/reset', so this review's only oracle was the unrelated skills-under-test plan. What a successor on evals/reset or a maintainer reviewing #1087 cannot find in the repo: (1) the O1-O5 owner questions, their alternatives and the O4/O5 veto window. kernel/numbers.py restates O4/O5's substance, but not that O5 reverses the earlier 'a whole value drops its decimal' rule. (2) The V1 coordinator token rulings (muted #5f6774/#8a93a1, the hue angles), which depart from the plan's own defaults (#646c79; 'blue, purple, magenta, teal-blue and brown'). (3) The V2 ruling that the All aspects row reads equal-weight means. This one is in MIGRATIONS.toml. (4) The V3 ruling that a change chart's level is the arm table's figure, plus its 'paired baseline on written pages' follow-up, which has no issue number. (5) The V4 reading of 'per 1,000 replies everywhere' as the input's own count noun. (6) The V5 descopes: one level pe